            "In many of the world's driest regions, the question is no longer whether water will become scarcer, but how societies will adapt when it does. Growing populations, expanding cities and rising temperatures all increase demand. On current projections, by the middle of the century demand in several arid regions will have grown substantially, while traditional sources, particularly underground aquifers that took thousands of years to fill, are being used far faster than they are replaced.",
            "For countries with long coastlines and access to energy, the most visible response has been desalination. The Gulf region alone produces a large share of the world's desalinated water, and modern plants supply most of the drinking water in several of its cities. Technology has improved considerably. Reverse osmosis, which pushes seawater through fine membranes, uses far less energy than the older method of boiling and condensing, and its costs have fallen steadily for decades.",
            "Yet desalination is not a complete solution. It remains energy-intensive, and where that energy comes from fossil fuels, it adds to the emissions that are making the region hotter. It also produces brine, highly concentrated salty water that is usually returned to the sea. In enclosed, shallow seas, scientists have warned that rising salinity near outflows could affect marine life, although the scale of the risk depends heavily on how and where the brine is released. Increasingly, new plants are being designed to run on solar power and to spread their discharge more widely.",
            "Cost is a further consideration, though a more complicated one than it first appears. The price of producing desalinated water has fallen, but the full cost includes the pipelines and pumping needed to move water from the coast to inland cities, sometimes hundreds of kilometres away and uphill. Large plants also take years to plan and build, which means that decisions made today determine supply a decade from now. Governments that wait until shortages are visible may find that the solutions they need cannot be delivered in time.",
            "A second response receives less attention but is often cheaper: using existing water more wisely. In some cities, a significant proportion of treated water is lost through leaking pipes before it ever reaches a tap. Repairing infrastructure, installing smart meters that detect leaks, and pricing water so that heavy users pay more can all reduce demand without building anything new. Agriculture, which in many dry countries uses most of the available fresh water, offers the largest opportunity. Drip irrigation, which delivers water directly to plant roots, can cut use dramatically compared with flooding fields.",
            "A third approach treats wastewater not as waste but as a resource. Treated sewage water is already widely used to irrigate parks and public landscapes, and some cities have begun to use advanced treatment to return it to the drinking supply. The technology works; the obstacle is often public acceptance. Surveys suggest that people's willingness to drink recycled water depends less on scientific explanations than on trust in the authorities who manage it, and on the language used to describe it. In one city, residents were far more willing to accept the idea when it was described as 'purified water' rather than 'recycled sewage', even though the process was exactly the same.",
            "None of these responses is sufficient on its own, and each involves trade-offs. Desalination provides reliable supply but at an environmental and energy cost. Conservation is cheap but requires changes in behaviour and pricing that can be politically difficult, particularly where water has long been provided at very low prices. Recycling is efficient but depends on public confidence that can take years to build.",
